{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Change 2623 patchset 1 came back -1 on BOTH gates. The two have unrelated causes and only\none is a code fault. Recording both so the next session does not re-derive them.\n\n## Verified -1 is BASE DRIFT, not a code fault -- and NOT a recheck candidate\n\nCONFIRMED, not inferred. 2623's parent is 3ae5c7a6082c (\"Name make verify as the pre-push\nCI contract\"); `git show 3ae5c7a6082c:pyproject.toml | grep -c grounding-terraform` returns\n0, while c57057f41 (\"Enable the grounding-terraform extra\") adds it. All four matrix legs\nfailed uniformly in 40-71s at the install step -- the same fast, uniform, install-step\nsignature that bug 1ef8's patchset 1 hit hours earlier and diagnosed.\n\nRemedy is a REBASE onto current main, not `recheck`: the tree is fine, the base predates a\ndependency the build now requires. Per AGENTS.md, `recheck` is reserved for provably\nenvironmental faults and this is not one -- it is reproducible from the base commit.\n\nTwo changes hitting the identical drift in one evening suggests the window between cutting\na worktree and pushing is now long enough (a 15-20 min plan review plus a full local suite)\nthat a pyproject-touching commit can land underneath. Worth a ticket if it recurs a third\ntime; not filing one on n=2.\n\n## LLM-Review -1 is ONE REAL BLOCKING CORRECTNESS FINDING, and it hits the story's crux\n\ninfra/scripts/observability.sh:296. `docker_unaccounted_bytes` subtracts the FULL\n`docker system df` ledger -- Images + Containers + Build Cache -- from a `du` of overlay2\nALONE. But BuildKit build cache lives under /var/lib/docker/buildkit, NOT under overlay2.\n\nSo the subtrahend counts bytes the minuend never included, and the residue is\nsystematically UNDER-reported by the size of the build cache. That is precisely the metric\nthis story exists to publish: the whole design rests on differencing two independent\nmeasurements of the same bytes, and the two measurements do not currently cover the same\nbytes. A large build cache could mask real orphaned-overlay2 residue entirely, or drive the\nfigure negative.\n\nI previously reported this crux as working. It does not; the reviewer is right and the\narithmetic needs to be reconciled before this lands. Either widen the `du` to cover\nbuildkit as well, or narrow the ledger to Images + Containers -- but the two sides must be\nmade to span the same byte set, and whichever is chosen needs a test that would fail under\nthe other.\n\n## Five advisories, two of which are substantive\n\n- docker-storage-cap.sh:251 -- on a re-run against an already-running daemon, the script\n  reports the new builder.gc policy is \"in effect\" after a successful `systemctl reload\n  docker`, but Docker's SIGHUP reload does NOT apply builder.gc / storage GC settings.\n  This is the SAME meta-pattern as 9a17, 90c7, and 1ef8's own vacuous AC3: ABSENCE OF\n  EXECUTION REPORTED AS SUCCESS. Fourth instance this session. A cap that reports itself\n  installed but is not in force is worse than no cap, for the same reason the ticket's own\n  description gives about partial refusals: the loud half creates confidence.\n- docker-storage-cap.sh:235 -- every non-no-op `--install` writes a timestamped\n  daemon.json.bak.$(date +%s) and never prunes them, so repeated installs accumulate\n  unbounded backups. Notable because this is a DISK-CEILING story growing an unbounded\n  on-disk set.\n- observability.sh:318 -- docker_storage_used_percent and docker_buildkit_cache_used_percent\n  are unclamped integers, so exceeding budget publishes >100. The 85 threshold still fires,\n  so this is presentation, not alarm correctness.\n- test_docker_storage_cap.py:397-411 -- asserts a VERBATIM source line of the script under\n  test and string-slices around it. Change-detector; would break under a behaviour-preserving\n  edit. Rewrite against observable behaviour.\n- test_observability_docker_storage.py:232-253 -- the awk ledger parser fails the whole read\n  on an unrecognised size suffix.\n\n## Status\n\nNOT dispatched -- this session is paused at the operator's instruction. 2623 stays NEW and\nunsubmitted. The blocking finding is a genuine design correction, not a nit, so this needs a\nreal fix pass rather than a rebase-and-repush. Stories S3 (e956), S4 (2ba3) and S5 (910b)\nremain held behind it on the shared compose-up.sh / observability.sh /\nmonitoring_autodeploy.tf / review-bot-ops.md file set."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788539013097257001,"uuid":"fe70d01a-8dd2-4e69-9183-50941bd70d2d"}